// Translation fragment: featureHelp (en) — the detailed help window behind the "i" button
// in SessionFeatures.svelte. featureHelp.<key>.body is paragraphs separated by a blank line;
// `backticks` render as code. Keys match FeatureItem.key in frontend/src/lib/sessionFeatures.ts.

export default {
    "featureHelp.open": "More: how it works, with an example",
    "featureHelp.example": "Example",
    "featureHelp.close": "Close",

    "featureHelp.taskSource.body": "The task queue is a file in the project (`task_source`, a path relative to the project folder) the manager uses to track the session's work. Each open task is one pointer line like `ROADMAP.md:92` (a file and the line number holding the task's description). The old format with «In progress:» / «Next:» markers is also supported.\n\nThe file itself is not handed to the agent: the session's prompt tells it what to take from the queue (for example, «follow the protocol in docs/git-workflow.md»). The manager uses the file like this: before a run it shows the top task's description in the UI (the line the pointer points to, up to 200 characters); after the turn it checks whether the top pointer left the queue. The queue is read from `origin/<main branch>` after a `git fetch`, or from disk if there is no remote.\n\nPointer gone — the task counts as done. Still there, but the agent ended with the «continue in a new session» marker — a slice is done and the next session continues. Still there with no marker — the task is unfinished; after 3 such runs in a row the loop stops with an error so it does not keep paying for the same thing forever.\n\nThe queue makes sense together with `auto_restart` (take tasks one after another); when the queue is empty the session stops (unless `run_without_tasks` is on). If a queue is set, pre-flight analysis is skipped for the session.",
    "featureHelp.taskSource.example": "Session «Developer 1», `task_source = \"STATUS-P1.md\"`, the file holds two lines: `RUNTIME-TASKS.md:40` and `RUNTIME-TASKS.md:75`. The UI shows line 40 of RUNTIME-TASKS.md. The agent finishes the task and removes its line from the queue on master — the manager counts the task and, with `auto_restart = true`, starts the next one, line 75.",
    "featureHelp.autoRestart.body": "Without auto-restart a session is one Claude process: once it finishes (or fails), the session stops. With `auto_restart = true` the manager runs a loop: one process — one task. After the turn it closes the process and immediately starts a new one with a clean context (a new conversation id), so the previous task is not dragged into the next one's context and does not make it more expensive.\n\nIn this (autonomous) mode you cannot type to the session mid-work: the process ends together with the turn. The agent asks you questions through a special block that the manager shows in the UI. After an error the next launch comes after `default_retry_delay` (30 s by default); after a rate limit — after a pause, or at once on the fallback model if one is configured.\n\n`max_tasks` caps the number of tasks per session start (0 — no limit). Only closed tasks count: a slice of a task or an unfinished run do not. Without `task_source`, auto-restart will launch the same prompt over and over — a badge in the list warns about this.",
    "featureHelp.autoRestart.example": "Session P1, `auto_restart = true`, `max_tasks = 3`, `task_source = \"STATUS-P1.md\"`. The session closes a task, a new process starts at once with the next one, and so on. After the third closed task the loop stops and the session goes idle, even if the queue still has tasks.",
    "featureHelp.runWithoutTasks.body": "By default, when the queue (`task_source`) has no tasks left, the session does not spin an empty loop. The manager checks the queue file before every process launch: with no tasks it starts a plain interactive session instead of the usual prompt — the agent is told the queue is empty, replies that it is ready and waits for your message. It has no auto-restart — stop it by hand when you are done. The log shows «No pending tasks in … — starting a plain interactive session».\n\n`run_without_tasks = true` turns this check off: the session launches with the same prompt again and again even on an empty queue. Only needed for queues whose format the manager does not recognise. It only works together with `task_source`.",
    "featureHelp.runWithoutTasks.example": "Session P1, `task_source = \"STATUS-P1.md\"`, `auto_restart = true`, everything else default. The agent closes the last task and removes the last line from the queue. On the next round the manager sees an empty file and, instead of a new run, opens a chat: you can give the agent a one-off job or just stop the session.",
    "featureHelp.useWorktree.body": "With `use_worktree = true` the manager starts Claude with the `--worktree` flag: the CLI itself creates a separate git worktree — a second working folder of the same repository on its own branch — and works there. This way two sessions in one project do not edit the same files at the same time.\n\nIf `worktree_name` is empty, every process launch creates a new anonymous worktree from the current HEAD. In an autonomous loop this means each task gets a fresh copy, and uncommitted work of an interrupted run stays in the old one, invisible to the next run. If a name is set, the CLI uses the worktree with that name, which may lag behind the main branch.\n\nThe cost of this is measured in the repo: on 2026-09-08 the same task was done seven times in seven anonymous worktrees, none of the attempts reached master — about $12 (docs/design-decisions.md). That is why developer sessions, which manage persistent worktree slots themselves by protocol, have this flag off. Task hooks and queue reads still run in the main project folder, not in the worktree.",
    "featureHelp.useWorktree.example": "Two sessions in project lumen: «Chat» without a worktree and «Refactor» with `use_worktree = true`, `worktree_name = \"refactor\"`. «Refactor» edits files in its own worktree folder on its own branch while you work in the main folder — the changes do not mix until the branch is merged.",
    "featureHelp.preflight.body": "Why: a big task handed to one session can eat the context and the budget and end half-done (PLAN.md §17). Pre-flight analysis asks a cheap model before the start whether one session can handle the task.\n\nHow it works: when you press ▶ in the session list, the manager runs an analyst (`preflight_model`, haiku by default, spend cap `preflight_max_budget`) on the session's prompt. In `auto` mode, if the task fits one session and `preflight_auto_approve_single` is on, the session starts at once. Otherwise a window with a plan (subtasks) opens: you can launch the plan or bypass it with «start the session as is». `always` mode always shows the plan, `never` skips the analysis.\n\nWhen it does nothing: with the global `preflight_analysis` off; for a session without a prompt; for a session with a queue (`task_source`) or `auto_restart` — it takes a new task each time. The analysis is also skipped when the whole project is started at once and when starting through MCP/control-plane — only the button in the UI triggers it.\n\nCost: an extra call to a cheap model and a wait of seconds to minutes before every start.",
    "featureHelp.preflight.example": "Session «Chat» with the prompt «Rewrite the auth module from JWT to OAuth2», `preflight = \"auto\"`. The analyst decides it will not fit one session and proposes a plan of 3 subtasks. You review it and either launch the plan or press «start as is». For the prompt «fix a typo in README» the session starts at once, with no window.",
    "featureHelp.taskHooks.body": "Hooks are your shell commands that the manager runs around the agent's work: `pre_task_hook` and `post_task_hook`. They run in the project folder via `cmd /C` (on Windows), with no time limit; their output goes to the session log, ending with «ok» or an exit code.\n\n`pre_task_hook` runs before every launch of the Claude process — so also before a retry after an error and before a restart caused by a full context. If it fails, the agent is not launched: this counts as a failed run, and with `auto_restart` the manager retries after `default_retry_delay`; without it the session stops.\n\n`post_task_hook` runs only after a closed task — not after a slice of a task, an unfinished run or an error. Its failure is only logged and does not affect the loop.\n\nCost: the hook's time is added to every task; a long or hung hook holds the session until it ends.",
    "featureHelp.taskHooks.example": "Session P1, `pre_task_hook = \"git pull -q\"`, `post_task_hook = \"go build ./...\"`. Before every run the project folder pulls fresh code; if the pull fails (for example, a conflict), the agent is not launched and a retry follows in 30 s. After every closed task the build runs, and its output is visible in the log.",
    "featureHelp.crashRecovery.body": "If the app closes, crashes or the computer shuts down mid-task, without this setting the agent's conversation would be lost and the task would have to start from scratch.\n\nHow it works: before every process launch the manager writes `~/.claude-manager/state/<project>-<session>.json` with the start time and the current task, and as soon as Claude reports its conversation id, adds that too. After a successfully finished turn the file is deleted. A file left behind with an id means the previous run was interrupted. Then pressing ▶ opens a window: «Continue» (Claude starts with `--resume`, i.e. with the previous conversation history), «Start fresh» (the file is deleted) or «Cancel». A session with such a file is marked ⏸ in the list.\n\nOn continue the agent gets a message: check `git status`, re-read the task queue and work out from the conversation history and the repository state what is already done — instead of trusting its own last message. You can replace this text (`crash_recovery_prompt` on the session). When the whole project is started at once or through MCP, an interrupted session is continued automatically — there is nobody to ask.\n\nThe setting is global (`crash_recovery`, on by default). When off, no state files are written at all. Cost: continuing loads the whole previous history, which is more expensive than a fresh start.",
    "featureHelp.crashRecovery.example": "Session P1 was working on task RUNTIME-TASKS.md:40 and the computer rebooted. After the manager starts, P1 shows ⏸ in the list. You press ▶, see «interrupted: RUNTIME-TASKS.md:40, started at 14:05» and choose «Continue» — the agent checks git status and finishes the task in the same conversation.",
    "featureHelp.contextRestart.body": "From the study (docs/Science_lab/MEASURE.md): every model call re-reads the whole conversation, so the longer a session runs, the more each next step costs. Sessions over 100 calls are about a fifth of all sessions but 55–56% of all spend. This setting keeps a conversation from growing to the limit of the context window.\n\nHow it works: after every turn the manager checks what share of the context window that turn used. The first time the share reaches `context_restart_threshold` (0.75 by default), `context_restart_mode` kicks in. `resume` (the default) — `/compact` is sent into the same process and Claude compresses the history itself. `fresh` — a cheap model (haiku) writes a short recap from the transcript and the todo list, the process is closed, and a new process starts from that recap. `ask` — a question appears with buttons: compact, restart with a recap, or continue as is. `off` — nothing.\n\nWorth knowing: the check runs only between turns — the turn that crossed the threshold completes, the next one is what changes. Autonomous sessions (`auto_restart`/queue) and the Hermes runtime always use `fresh`: they have no live process waiting for the next message. A context restart does not count as a closed task and does not run `post_task_hook`.\n\nTurned on per session (`context_handoff`, off by default); mode and threshold are global in `[optimization]`. Cost: a recap loses details, and both `/compact` and the recap cost a model call of their own.",
    "featureHelp.contextRestart.example": "Interactive session «Chat», `context_handoff = true`, `context_restart_mode = \"ask\"`, threshold 0.75, a 200,000-token window. A turn used 152,000 tokens (76%) — the question «Context usage reached 76%…» appears. You choose «Compact this conversation», and the next turn starts with the compressed history. The same setting on autonomous session P1 would, without asking, restart it with a short recap.",
    "featureHelp.handoffContextTokens.body": "Research finding (docs/Science_lab/MEASURE.md): every model call re-reads the whole conversation, so the cost of a call grows with the length of the session. In Lumen, sessions over 100 calls were about 23% of sessions but 56% of all spend; the average context per call there was 117k tokens.\n\nThis is implemented as `handoff_context_tokens`. The limit is not on the number of calls but on the context size, which is what sets the price. The manager measures the context of every call (input tokens plus tokens read from and written to the cache). When a call exceeds the budget, the manager sends the agent a tagged message mid-turn: finish the current step, commit the work with a note saying what is done, what is next and how to verify it, and end the turn with the `continue_session` marker. A new session with a small context continues the task. If the agent does not comply, the reminder is repeated after 20 more over-budget calls, at most 3 messages per run.\n\nIt works only in autonomous sessions (`auto_restart`) and only with the Claude runtime; in interactive sessions and with Hermes it does nothing. 0 means off, and then the prompt does not change by a single byte.\n\nThe cost: a task is split across several sessions, and each new one gets to know the project again. Enabled on 2026-10-13 at 150000 for autonomous sessions (Lumen S1–S6, claude-manager \"Developer 1\"); the \"after\" measurement has not been done yet.",
    "featureHelp.handoffContextTokens.example": "Budget 150000. On call 70 the agent sent the model 152k tokens of context, so the manager tells it the budget is reached. The agent finishes the test, commits to the task branch with the note \"done: parser; next: error handling; verify: go test ./internal/parser\" and ends the turn. The next session starts from scratch and continues from that note.",
    "featureHelp.contextPrimer.body": "A new session usually spends its first calls re-discovering what the manager already knows: which task, which branch, what changed last time. Each such call also grows the context, which is then re-read until the session ends.\n\nWith `context_primer = true` the manager adds a \"Project state (auto-generated)\" block before the first message of a fresh run. In order of priority it contains: the current task from `task_source`; git — the main branch, whether there is a remote and its name, the current branch and the last commits; the files the previous run of this session changed via Edit/Write; the project's check commands (gates); how long the project's slow commands actually take. The block is capped at 2000 characters — on overflow whole sections are dropped from the end, least important first.\n\nThe block is not added on crash recovery or when the queue has no tasks. The \"previous run files\" and \"command durations\" sections come from the action history, so they appear only with experience tracking (`experience_tracking`) on. If git is unavailable or hangs longer than 3 seconds, the git section is simply skipped — the session still starts.\n\nThe cost is up to 2000 characters (a few hundred tokens) at the start of every fresh run. When off, the prompt does not change by a single byte.",
    "featureHelp.contextPrimer.example": "The \"Developer 1\" session starts fresh. Before its prompt stands: \"Current task: STATUS-P1.md:12\", branch `p1-rt-03`, remote `origin`, the last three commits, the files `internal/session/parser.go` and `parser_test.go` from the previous run, and the line `Gate commands: go test ./...`. The agent does not need to run `git status` and `git log` to get its bearings.",
    "featureHelp.askUserMarker.body": "When the agent needs your decision, it usually asks in plain text and you have to type the answer. With `ask_user_marker = true` the manager adds a short instruction to an interactive session's system prompt: put the question in an ```ask-user``` block with JSON like {\"question\": ..., \"options\": [...]} and end the turn right away.\n\nThe manager recognizes the block and shows the question with option buttons. The session waits with no time limit; your choice (or any message you type) goes to the agent as the next turn.\n\nThe setting matters only for interactive sessions. Autonomous sessions (`auto_restart`) always get their own version of the protocol — there, if nobody answers within 5 minutes, the first option is chosen. The manager recognizes the block even with the setting off (for example, when the project's CLAUDE.md asks for it) — the setting only teaches the model the format. Works for both Claude and Hermes.\n\nThe cost is a few lines in the system prompt. When off, an interactive session's prompt does not change by a single byte.",
    "featureHelp.askUserMarker.example": "In a chat the agent reaches a fork and, instead of a paragraph of text, ends its turn with the block {\"question\": \"Where to store settings?\", \"options\": [\"TOML file\", \"SQLite\"]}. Two buttons appear in the session window; you click \"SQLite\" and the agent continues with that answer.",
    "featureHelp.systemPromptAppend.body": "Lets you give the agent standing instructions that hold for the whole run without repeating them in every message. The text from `system_prompt_append` is added to Claude's system prompt through the `--append-system-prompt` flag; Claude Code's built-in system prompt is not replaced.\n\nThe manager appends its own service instructions to the same flag: in autonomous sessions — the question protocol and the warning about background commands (and the handoff rule if `handoff_context_tokens` is set), in interactive ones — the button-question format if `ask_user_marker` is on. Your text comes first, the service text after it.\n\nThe Hermes runtime has no system prompt of this kind: there the same text is sent as a preamble before the first turn.\n\nThe cost: the text is part of the context of every model call, so long instructions get more expensive as the session grows. Instructions shared by all sessions of a project usually belong in the project's CLAUDE.md instead.",
    "featureHelp.systemPromptAppend.example": "Value: \"Answer in Russian. Do not change files under vendor/\". The session starts with `--append-system-prompt \"Answer in Russian. Do not change files under vendor/\"`, and the agent follows it for the whole run even if you never repeat it in the chat.",
    "featureHelp.addDirs.body": "By default a Claude Code agent works only inside the project folder. If a task needs files from somewhere else — a sibling repository, a shared library, a data folder — you can list them in `add_dirs`.\n\nEach non-empty entry is passed to Claude as a separate `--add-dir <folder>` flag, and the agent gets the same access to those folders as to the project. Permission prompts still work as usual, according to the session's permission mode.\n\nThe Hermes runtime does not support this setting: on launch it writes \"Hermes runtime ignores: add_dirs\" to the session log.\n\nThe cost: the agent can read and change files outside the project, so add only the folders the task really needs.",
    "featureHelp.addDirs.example": "The project is D:\\GolangProjects\\claude-manager, and the agent needs to check code in a neighbouring project. `add_dirs` contains `C:\\kostja\\Rust_Projects\\Lumen_browser`; the session starts with `--add-dir C:\\kostja\\Rust_Projects\\Lumen_browser`, and the agent can open files from there without extra questions about access to an outside folder.",
    "featureHelp.experienceTracking.body": "The foundation of the \"experience layer\": to find recurring agent actions and suggest improvements, the manager needs a history of what the agents did. Off by default — then the manager never opens transcripts at all.\n\nWith `experience_tracking = true` (a global setting, section `[optimization]`), after every run the manager reads the Claude Code transcript (or, if it cannot be found, the auto-saved session log) and stores one row per tool call in the local database: which tool, a generalized argument, whether it failed, the output size. It also records how each permission request was resolved — by a rule or by a person.\n\nThis history feeds: the \"Actions\" tab, permission-rule suggestions, skill autopilot (which does not work without tracking), the \"previous run files\" and \"command durations\" sections of the project state primer (`context_primer`), and the \"Import logs\" button — it loads a project's old logs into the database and returns an error when the setting is off.\n\nThe setting adds no model calls: it is local file reading and SQLite writes in the background, and it does not affect the outcome of a run. The price is disk space and the fact that the action history is stored on the machine.",
    "featureHelp.experienceTracking.example": "You turn tracking on and click \"Import logs\" for Lumen: its `.claude-manager/logs/` folder held 5791 files (245 MB), 946 of them importable runs. After the import, the \"Actions\" tab shows which commands the agents run most often and which of them fail most.",
    "featureHelp.journal.body": "The project journal is a short memory between sessions: after each task a record remains of what was done, what was unexpected and what to avoid, instead of reconstructing it from a diff.\n\nWith `journal = true`, after every successfully finished run (a task closed, or part of it handed to the next session) the manager makes one separate Claude call in the background — the model from `preflight_model`, haiku by default, in read-only mode. It gets the task pointer, the files the run changed via Edit/Write, and the agent's final text. The answer is written as a section to `<project>/.claude-manager/journal.md` with the date and the task. Empty items stay as a dash — the model is asked not to invent \"surprises\" for a routine task.\n\nThe file keeps the last 50 entries; older ones move to `journal-archive-YYYY-MM.md` by the entry's month. By default these files are added to `.gitignore` (see \"Commit the journal\"). Currently the manager does not insert the journal into the prompt itself — the agent or you read the file when needed.\n\nThe setting is per project and is stored in the private config layer rather than the shared one, so a colleague who clones the repository turns it on themselves. The cost is one extra model call after every finished task; a failure of that call does not affect the task itself.",
    "featureHelp.journal.example": "The agent closes task ROADMAP.md:92. A few seconds later journal.md gets:\n\"## 2026-10-13 — ROADMAP.md:92\n**Сделано:** settings parser added\n**Неожиданно:** tests need CRLF in fixtures\n**Не делать:** —\" (the section headings are always written in Russian).",
    "featureHelp.journalCommit.body": "By default the project journal is treated as local run history: on every write the manager adds `journal.md` and any archives it creates (`journal-archive-YYYY-MM.md`) to the `.gitignore` of the `.claude-manager` folder.\n\nWith `journal_commit = true` this step is skipped, and the journal files can be reviewed and committed like any other project document. The manager itself does not commit them — the agent or you do.\n\nWithout the journal turned on (`journal`) this setting does nothing. It does not remove lines already added to `.gitignore` — if the journal was kept without it before, you have to remove the line by hand.\n\nThe cost: journal entries end up in the repository history and are visible to anyone with access to it.",
    "featureHelp.journalCommit.example": "The team wants to see the journal in review. `journal` and `journal_commit` are on; after a task, `git status` shows a modified `.claude-manager/journal.md`, and the agent commits it together with the task's code.",
    "featureHelp.autoSkills.body": "Agents in one project keep repeating the same chains of actions and work them out from scratch every time. A skill is a `SKILL.md` file in `.claude/skills/` that an agent can load to know the steps right away. The autopilot (`auto_skills`) finds such repeats and turns them into skills on its own, without your involvement.\n\nAfter each run, once its transcript is indexed, the manager takes one step: it picks a frequently repeated pattern of actions, one model writes a draft skill, and a second model (opus by default) reviews it and filters out duplicates, trivial steps, generic advice and dangerous actions. An accepted skill is written into the project «on trial». Only one skill is on trial at a time, and there are at least 5 runs between two new drafts.\n\nAfter 10 runs the manager decides: if the skill was never loaded, it is archived; if it was loaded but the median input tokens grew by more than 20% or the share of completed runs dropped by 0.2 or more, it is archived; otherwise it is kept. A kept skill that has not been loaded for 20 runs in a row is archived too. Archiving moves the folder to `.claude-manager/archived-skills/`, and the skill can be restored from there.\n\nNothing works without `experience_tracking` (turning the autopilot on from the UI turns it on as well). The cost: paid calls for drafts and reviews — capped by `auto_skills_daily_budget_usd` (0 = no cap) — and files written straight into the project repository. That is why the flag lives in the private `config.local.toml`, not in the shared config.",
    "featureHelp.autoSkills.example": "In every task the project's agents manually build the frontend, run the tests and search the output for errors. The autopilot notices this pattern, writes a «build-and-test» skill, the reviewer accepts it, and the skill lands in `.claude/skills/`. Over the next 10 runs agents loaded it 6 times and the median token count did not grow, so the skill stays. With `auto_skills_daily_budget_usd = 2` the autopilot spends no more than $2 a day on drafts and reviews.",
    "featureHelp.mixedProgramming.body": "The idea comes from the lumen project: Claude prepares a detailed specification (a brief), and the code itself is written by external free models (for example Step 3.7 Flash and Nemotron 3 Ultra via Kilo Gateway). That way the expensive model spends tokens on framing the task, not on typing code.\n\nHow it works: in the «Mixed» window you pick a project, a worker (`[[worker]]`) and a brief. The external model sends edits in FIND/REPLACE format; the manager applies them in a separate git worktree and runs the project's check commands — the gates (`gates`, e.g. `go build ./...` and `go test ./...`). If the gates fail, the exact error output goes back to the model for the next round. A green result is committed in the worktree and waits for your review; after `mixed_max_rounds` (default 3) failed rounds the task is marked «needs human». Tests written by the model itself are not trusted — only the gates count.\n\nDispatch is manual: sessions do not hand work to external models on their own. The feature will not turn on without gates and at least one `[[worker]]` — a config with `mixed_programming = true` but without them fails validation. The worker's API key is read from an environment variable (`key_env`) and is never written to the config.\n\nThe main cost is privacy: the brief and verbatim excerpts of your code go to external free endpoints that log requests. That is why the flag is an explicit opt-in, off by default and stored in the private `config.local.toml`. Do not send confidential code this way.",
    "featureHelp.mixedProgramming.example": "The project has `gates = [\"go build ./...\", \"go test ./...\"]` and a worker `step37`. You send the brief «add a date-parsing function to internal/util». In round one `go test` fails and the manager returns the test output to the model; in round two the gates are green and the edit is committed to an `mp-…` branch in a separate worktree. You review it and merge it yourself.",
    "featureHelp.mirror.body": "Each project's settings live in its own folder, so an agent working in the claude-manager folder cannot see how another project is set up, what its sessions run or what their latest logs say. The mirror solves this: a copy of the project's settings and logs appears in the manager's folder.\n\nWith `mirror` on, the manager copies into `projects/<name>/` (or `mirror_dir`): `CLAUDE.md`, `.mcp.json`, `.claude/settings*.json`, the `.claude/hooks`, `skills`, `agents`, `commands` folders, the `.claude-manager/config*.toml` and `journal.md` files, saved run logs, the skill archive and each session's task queue file. Next to them it writes `effective-config.toml` — the final project config as the manager sees it. Sync runs 10 seconds after start, after every settings save, every `mirror_interval_min` minutes (default 15) and from the «Sync now» button.\n\nCopying is one-way: the manager writes nothing into the project folder, and files deleted in the project are deleted from the copy too. The mirror folder is a separate local git repository with no remote: every change is committed there, so the history of settings is kept but never sent anywhere. Files over 5 MB are skipped.\n\nThe flag is stored only in the manager's global settings, not in the project folder. The cost is disk space for copies of logs. Editing the copy is pointless: fix things in the real project folder.",
    "featureHelp.mirror.example": "You turn the mirror on for the lumen project. Within 15 minutes `projects/lumen/` holds its `CLAUDE.md`, `effective-config.toml` and the logs of the latest runs. Now you can ask an agent in claude-manager «look at why lumen's P1 session keeps failing» — it reads the logs from the copy and makes the fix in the real lumen folder.",
    "featureHelp.permissionMode.body": "The permission mode decides whether the agent may run commands and edit files on its own or must ask. The `permission_mode` value is passed to Claude CLI as `--permission-mode`: `default`, `acceptEdits`, `auto`, `plan`, `dontAsk` or `bypassPermissions`. New sessions get `bypassPermissions` by default (or the project's `default_permission_mode`, if set).\n\nWhen the CLI sends a permission request, the manager first checks the rules (the session's `permission_rule` entries and rules added with the «allow for session/always» buttons). If no rule matches and the mode is not `bypassPermissions`, the request is shown in the UI, the session goes into «waiting for permission» and stays there until you answer. After `permission_notify_after` seconds (default 30) a reminder fires; `permission_timeout` (0 = wait forever) with `permission_timeout_action` decide what happens when nobody answers: deny, or stop the session.\n\nImportant for autonomous sessions (`auto_restart`): as checked on 2026-07-24, in `acceptEdits` mode Bash commands not listed in `allowed_tools` are denied silently and the request never reaches the manager — the session just burns tokens and stalls. So a strict mode makes sense where you are watching, or together with a list of allowed tools. Hermes understands only `bypassPermissions` (as `--yolo`); it ignores the other modes and applies its own policy to dangerous commands.\n\nThe trade-off: with `bypassPermissions` the agent never waits, but it also never asks before dangerous actions; strict modes are safer but need you present or rules set up in advance.",
    "featureHelp.permissionMode.example": "A «Chat» session with `permission_mode = \"default\"` wants to run `git push`. No rule covers it, so a request appears in the UI and the session waits. With `permission_timeout = 600` and `permission_timeout_action = \"deny\"`, after 10 minutes without an answer the manager denies it and the agent carries on without the push.",
    "featureHelp.permissionRules.body": "Rules spare you the same «may I?» questions and forbid dangerous things in advance. There are two kinds, and they work in different places.\n\n`allowed_tools` and `disallowed_tools` are passed to Claude CLI (`--allowedTools` / `--disallowedTools`) and enforced by the CLI itself. The format is `Tool(prefix:*)`: for example, `Bash(go:*)` allows any command starting with `go`. This is the main way to give an autonomous session in a strict mode the commands it needs: without them, in `acceptEdits` mode for instance, Bash commands outside the list are denied silently.\n\n`[[permission_rule]]` is checked by the manager itself when the CLI sends it a permission request. A rule has three fields: `tool` (`Bash`, `Edit`, `Write` or `*`), `pattern` (a pattern with `*` for the command or path; `*.go` also matches `src/main.go`) and `decision` (`allow`, `deny`, `ask`). Rules are checked in order and the first match wins; `ask` means «ask me».\n\nWith `bypassPermissions` rules are hardly needed: the agent is allowed everything anyway. Hermes ignores `allowed_tools`/`disallowed_tools`. The cost of an overly broad rule is that the agent does something without asking that you would have wanted to check, so allow with narrow patterns.",
    "featureHelp.permissionRules.example": "A Go project in `acceptEdits` mode has `allowed_tools = [\"Bash(go:*)\", \"Bash(gofmt:*)\"]` and `disallowed_tools = [\"Bash(rm -rf *)\"]`. On top of that there is a manager rule `tool = \"Bash\"`, `pattern = \"rm *\"`, `decision = \"deny\"`. The session runs `go test ./...` freely, and any request for `rm` is denied without involving you.",
    "featureHelp.maxBudget.body": "The limit protects against one session quietly burning a lot of money: it got stuck in a loop, took on too large a task or bloated its context.\n\nThe manager passes `max_budget_usd` to Claude CLI as the `--max-budget-usd` flag on every launch of the CLI process; the CLI itself tracks the total and stops. The limit applies to one launch, not to the session over its whole life: in an autonomous session with `auto_restart` each next task launches the CLI again and the count starts from zero.\n\n0 means «no limit» — then the flag is not passed at all. Hermes sessions do not support this setting, and the manager notes that in the log.\n\nThe trade-off: a limit set too low cuts a task off midway, and what was spent before the cut is wasted. Set the limit well above a task's usual cost — it is insurance against an accident, not a way to save money.",
    "featureHelp.maxBudget.example": "A typical P1 task costs $1–2. You set `max_budget_usd = 5`. If the agent gets stuck on some task and spends $5, the CLI stops, and with `auto_restart` on the next task starts with a fresh $5 limit.",
    "featureHelp.fallbackModel.body": "When the main model hits its rate limit, the session normally stands and waits: until the reset time reported by the CLI (plus 5 seconds), or for `rate_limit_pause` seconds (default 300, i.e. 5 minutes). A fallback model lets it skip the wait.\n\nThe setting has two fields. `fallback_model` on its own is passed to Claude CLI as the `--fallback-model` flag — the CLI itself uses it when the model is overloaded. `fallback_model_on_rate_limit = true` adds the manager's behaviour: if a run ended on a rate limit, the manager immediately restarts the session on `fallback_model`, with no pause. The switch is one-way: the manager does not switch back to the main model on its own.\n\nWithout `fallback_model` filled in, the flag does nothing. Hermes ignores `fallback_model` — there the fallback is set up via `fallback_providers` in the Hermes profile.\n\nThe cost: another model continues the work — usually a weaker one or one with a different price — and the quality of the rest of the task may differ.",
    "featureHelp.fallbackModel.example": "A session runs on `opus` with `fallback_model = \"sonnet\"` and `fallback_model_on_rate_limit = true`. Midway through a task opus hits its rate limit. Instead of a 5-minute pause the manager immediately restarts the session on sonnet, and the task continues on it.",
    "featureHelp.loopDetection.body": "Guards against loops: the agent reads the same file or runs the same command again and again, spending money without progress. PLAN.md estimates (an estimate, not a measurement) one such case at $0.50–5.00.\n\nHow it works: the manager remembers the last `loop_window` tool calls (20 by default) and fires when the same tool with the same input appeared `loop_threshold` times (3 by default). Inputs are compared by meaning: for Bash — the first 80 characters of the command; for Read — the path and the range read (reading a large file in parts is not a repeat); for Edit — the path and the start of the replaced text (different edits of one file are not a repeat). With `loop_ignore_read_after_edit` reading a file right after editing it does not count either.\n\nWhat happens on a hit is `loop_action`: `warn` — a line in the session log and a toast; `send_hint` — the same, plus the `loop_hint` text is sent to the agent; `restart` — the same, then the run is stopped and starts over with a note about the loop in front of the prompt. If the restarted run loops again it only warns — no endless restarts. Hermes has no live input, so there `send_hint` works as `warn`.\n\nTrade-off: false alarms are possible (three identical `go test` runs are a «repeat» too), which is why the default is `warn`. `restart` loses the agent's train of thought, and «semantic» loops made of different calls are not caught.",
    "featureHelp.loopDetection.example": "The agent runs `grep -r TODO . --include=*.go` three times within its last 20 calls. With `loop_action = \"warn\"` the log shows «Possible loop: `Bash grep -r TODO …` repeated 3 times in the last 20 tool calls», and a toast appears. With `\"send_hint\"` the agent also gets «You seem to be repeating the same actions. Try a different approach.». With `\"restart\"` the run stops and starts again with a note «do not repeat that, check git status and continue differently».",
    "featureHelp.autoModelRouting.body": "Tasks of different difficulty do not need the same model. By the estimate in PLAN.md §20.3 (strategy 4), Haiku is about 20× cheaper than Sonnet and Opus about 5× more expensive, so giving Opus a file rename is overpaying.\n\nWith `auto_model_routing` on, clicking ▶ on a session does not start it right away. First the manager runs a short one-shot analysis of the session's prompt (model from `preflight_model`, haiku by default), gets a complexity estimate and turns it into a recommendation: trivial → haiku/low, standard → sonnet/medium, complex → sonnet/high, architectural → opus/high. If the project's history shows a cheaper lower-tier model with at least 5 runs of the same complexity, at least 90% of them completed and a lower average cost, the advice is lowered to that model. Architectural tasks are never downgraded. The recommendation appears in the model picker: accept it, change it, or skip the analysis and choose by hand.\n\nIt only works for a manual start with ▶ and only for a session that has a prompt; without a prompt the picker goes straight to manual choice. If pre-flight is on for the session and returned a plan, the plan review opens instead of the picker. Autonomous restarts, starting a whole project and crash recovery use the model from config. The ▶ button reads the setting when clicked, so a change applies at once.\n\nCost: every start pays for one extra model call (PLAN.md estimates $0.03–0.15) and a few seconds of waiting. The analysis can misjudge complexity, so the final choice is yours.",
    "featureHelp.autoModelRouting.example": "The «linter» session with the prompt «fix formatting in internal/» is configured for sonnet. You click ▶, the analysis rates the task as trivial, and the picker suggests haiku/low. You accept, and the session starts on haiku even though the config still says sonnet.",
    "featureHelp.maxTurnsAutoAdjust.body": "A looping run can go round in circles for dozens of turns and spend money with no result. PLAN.md §20.3 (strategy 5) gives an example: a normal run is 15 turns and about $1.50, a looping one 80+ turns and $8+.\n\nWith `max_turns_auto_adjust` on, before every Claude launch the manager looks at up to 20 recent finished runs of this session, takes the longest by turn count and multiplies it by 1.6 (rounded up). The result is passed to the CLI as `--max-turns`. The cap is never below `max_turns_default` (50 by default): it exists to stop a run that went far past the usual, not to trim normal work.\n\nUntil the session has 5 finished runs there is no cap at all. With the setting off, `--max-turns` is not passed at all — `max_turns_default` on its own limits nothing. It applies to Claude only; Hermes has its own `hermes_max_turns`. A new value takes effect at the next launch.\n\nA run that hits the cap ends as «unfinished»: a session with a task queue continues the task in its next run, a plain session simply stops. The risk: a task that genuinely needs more turns gets cut and continued in parts.",
    "featureHelp.maxTurnsAutoAdjust.example": "Among session P1's recent runs the longest took 40 turns. Cap = 40 × 1.6 = 64, so a run that starts looping stops at turn 64 instead of going on indefinitely. Had the longest been 18 turns, the result would be 29, but the floor `max_turns_default` = 50 would apply.",
    "featureHelp.excludeDynamicSystemPrompt.body": "By default Claude Code puts details of the current machine into the system prompt: working folder, environment, git status. That makes the prompt different for every session and even every restart, so the prompt cache misses and each launch pays again for a cache write.\n\nWith `exclude_dynamic_system_prompt` on (the default), the manager launches Claude with `--exclude-dynamic-system-prompt-sections`. Those details move from the system prompt into the first user message, the system prompt becomes identical across sessions, and later launches read it from the cache instead of writing it again. By the estimate in PLAN.md §20.3 (strategy 2), for 4 sessions with 10 restarts a day that is roughly $2.24 → $0.26 on the system prompt alone.\n\nIt pays off when there are many launches on the same model: several parallel sessions or frequent restarts. For one short session it makes almost no difference. Switching models resets the shared cache. The flag is passed to Claude only, not Hermes, and is read at session start — a running session needs a restart.\n\nCost: each launch's first message gets longer (PLAN.md estimates about 5k tokens); the agent's behaviour is not affected.",
    "featureHelp.excludeDynamicSystemPrompt.example": "Four sessions of one project on sonnet restart 10 times a day each. Without the flag each of the 40 launches writes its own system prompt into the cache; with it only the first one writes, the rest read the existing cache, which is about 12× cheaper.",
    "featureHelp.sessionStartDelay.body": "The prompt cache is created on the first call to the model. If several sessions start at once, none of them sees a neighbour's cache and each pays to write it. By the estimate in PLAN.md §20.3 (strategy 3): 4 sessions at once cost $0.24, with a 3-second pause $0.075.\n\nThe manager keeps one shared «start schedule» across all projects: each new session launch (by button, by starting a whole project, by crash recovery) waits until `session_start_delay` seconds (3 by default) have passed since the previous start. If the previous start was long ago there is no wait. The value is read live and applies to the next start. 0 means everything starts at once.\n\nIt only helps together with the shared system prompt (`exclude_dynamic_system_prompt`) and when sessions use the same model; otherwise there is no shared cache. A cache hit is not guaranteed — that depends on Anthropic's side.\n\nCost is waiting: with 8 sessions and a 3-second pause the last one starts after 21 seconds.",
    "featureHelp.sessionStartDelay.example": "You start a project with 4 sessions and `session_start_delay = 3`. The first starts at once, the second after 3 seconds, the third after 6, the fourth after 9. The second and later ones find the system prompt the first one already cached.",
    "featureHelp.pythonUtf8.body": "On a Russian-localized Windows the console uses the CP866 code page, and Python started by the agent prints in it by default. The agent gets garbled text instead of Russian, cannot read the output and spends calls on retries. According to the study (docs/Science_lab/MEASURE.md), without this setting 8.6% of Python runs in Lumen's sessions (45 of 523) returned unreadable output.\n\nHow it works: when each Claude session starts, the manager adds `PYTHONUTF8=1` and `PYTHONIOENCODING=utf-8` to its environment. The agent's Bash tool inherits that environment, so any Python the agent runs prints UTF-8. A variable that is already set (system-wide or in the session's environment) is left alone.\n\nIt applies from the session's start: a running session needs a restart. On by default, no extra cost. It does not change the output of other console programs (`tasklist`, `netstat`) — they still print CP866.",
    "featureHelp.pythonUtf8.example": "The agent runs `python parse_logs.py`, and the script prints «Ошибок: 3». With `python_utf8 = true` the agent sees exactly that text. Without it the agent sees something like «ЋиЁЎ®Є: 3», tries to work out what broke and spends extra calls on it.",
    "featureHelp.commandGuard.body": "A study of the saved run logs (7,010 sessions, 150k tool calls) found that a common slip is a `cd` into a folder the agent is already in — usually re-entering its own worktree, `cd .claude/worktrees/p1-work` from inside it. The command fails, and the agent spends one more call on the retry.\n\nWith `command_guard` on, the manager adds a hook to every Claude session. Before a Bash command that contains `cd .…` runs, the hook checks it: if the folder does not exist from the current directory but does exist under the project root, the path is made absolute. In the same pass it fixes Windows paths that bash mangles: `\"D:\\dir\\\"` (the last backslash swallows the quote) and an unquoted `D:\\dir`. On 93,000 commands from the logs the rules never changed a command that worked.\n\nThe hook does not start for other commands, so it costs nothing on them. The fix applies only in `bypassPermissions` mode: in other modes it would skip your permission prompt. Every run is written to `~/.claude-manager/logs/cmdguard.log`. Applies from the session's next start.",
    "featureHelp.commandGuard.example": "The agent is inside `.claude/worktrees/p1-work` and runs `cd .claude/worktrees/p1-work && cargo test` again. Without the guard: «No such file or directory» and a retry. With it the command runs as `cd \"D:/project/.claude/worktrees/p1-work\" && cargo test` and succeeds at once.",
    "featureHelp.envCard.body": "The agent does not know which machine it runs on and guesses: it writes `python` where only `python3` exists, calls `rev`, which Git Bash does not have, keeps scratch files in `/tmp` that Windows Python does not see, ends a quoted path with a backslash. Each guess that fails costs a call.\n\nWith `env_card` on, the manager adds 6-8 lines to every Claude session's system prompt: the OS and its shell rules, tools that are missing, the Python command that actually works (on Windows it searches the standard install folders when `python` on PATH is the Microsoft Store stub), the project root, and the rule for reading large files.\n\nThe text depends only on the machine and the project, so it is the same on every run and does not spoil the prompt cache. Cost: about 300 tokens in the cached system prompt. Applies from the session's next start.",
    "featureHelp.envCard.example": "On Windows the card says: «The Bash tool runs Git Bash: write Windows paths with forward slashes… Not installed in this shell: `rev`, `bc`. Python: call it by full path `C:/…/python.exe`.» The agent writes `D:/dir/` and the right Python from the first call.",
    "featureHelp.managerCard.body": "A session does not know it runs inside Claude Manager: \"create tasks and assign them to Developer 1\" reads to it as a Jira assignee, so it asks back. The card in the system prompt lists the project's sessions with their queues (task source) and the protocol: a task is a section of `<BLOCK>-TASKS.md` with Depends on / Files / What to do / Done when; size is one branch and one context window; assigning is a `file:line` pointer in the session's queue. A queue session also learns how to take and close a task.",
    "featureHelp.managerCard.example": "Chat in project ARB gets \"create tasks and assign them to developer 1\" and writes a `## KM-01: …` section into `KM-TASKS.md` and the pointer `KM-TASKS.md:12` into Developer 1's queue. If that session has no queue it creates `STATUS-DEVELOPER-1.md` and asks you to set it as the Task source.",
    "featureHelp.permissionNotify.body": "A session waiting for your permission to act stands still until you answer. If the manager window is minimised or you are in another program, this is easy to miss.\n\nWith `permission_native_notification` on (the default), every new permission request that reaches you shows a Windows system notification with the session name and the tool it wants to use. It is shown whether or not the window is open. The setting is read at the moment of the request, so a change applies at once, also to sessions already running.\n\nThere is no notification when the request is settled without you: by the session's permission rules, by «allow for this session / always» rules, or because the session runs in `bypassPermissions` mode. The notification itself allows nothing — you still answer in the manager window.",
    "featureHelp.permissionNotify.example": "Session «lumen/P1» wants to run a Bash command while you are in the browser. A Windows notification appears in the corner of the screen saying P1 is waiting for permission for Bash. You switch to the manager and answer.",
    "featureHelp.permissionSound.body": "The sound adds to the permission-request notification: you hear it even when you are not looking at the screen, while the session is standing still.\n\nWith `permission_sound` on (the default), every new permission request that reaches you plays two short rising tones in the manager window. The setting is read at the moment of the request and applies at once, also to sessions already running. It is independent of the Windows notification: you can keep only the sound or only the notification.\n\nThere is no sound when the request is settled without you (permission rules or `bypassPermissions` mode). The sound is played by the app window itself, not by the system, so it follows the volume and is silent when sound is muted.",
    "featureHelp.permissionSound.example": "You are reading documentation, not looking at the screen. Session «docs» asks for permission to write a file and two short tones play — you come back and answer without waiting to notice the request visually.",
    "featureHelp.permissionTimeout.body": "An unanswered permission request holds a session for as long as it takes: if you have left, it just waits. The timeout lets the request be settled automatically.\n\nWith `permission_timeout` above 0, the manager counts that many seconds from the moment the request appears. If you still have not answered by then, `permission_timeout_action` applies: `deny` — the request is refused, the agent gets the refusal and carries on without that action; `pause_session` — the request is dropped and the session is stopped in a way that lets it be continued later. A line about what happened is written to the session log. If you answered in time, the timeout does nothing. 0 means wait forever (the default).\n\nThe timeout only covers requests that reached you: those settled by rules or by `bypassPermissions` mode do not start it. The value is taken when the request appears, so a change applies from the next request.\n\nTrade-off: `deny` keeps the session from idling, but the agent may take a workaround or leave the task unfinished; `pause_session` is safer, but work stops until you return.",
    "featureHelp.permissionTimeout.example": "`permission_timeout = 300`, `permission_timeout_action = \"deny\"`. At night a session asks for permission to run `git push` while you are asleep. After 5 minutes the request is refused, the agent gets the refusal and continues with the rest of the work, and the log keeps a record of the automatic denial.",
    "featureHelp.budgetAlerts.body": "Parallel and autonomous sessions spend money while you are not watching. The alerts tell you when spending for the day or the week has passed a set amount.\n\nAfter every finished run (completed, unfinished, or a slice of a task) the manager adds up the spend of all projects for today and for the last 7 days in local time. If today's total reaches `daily_budget_alert`, or the 7-day total reaches `weekly_budget_alert`, a Windows notification appears with the current total and the threshold. Each alert is shown once: the daily one once a day, the weekly one once per calendar week. 0 turns an alert off (both are 0 by default). The thresholds are read live and apply from the next run.\n\nIt is only a warning: sessions are not stopped or slowed. The check runs when a run finishes, so one long run can overshoot the threshold noticeably before the alert arrives. To hard-cap the spend of a single run there is a separate setting, `max_budget_usd`.",
    "featureHelp.budgetAlerts.example": "`daily_budget_alert = 20`, `weekly_budget_alert = 100`. By lunchtime three sessions have spent $18.40, then a run of «lumen/P2» finishes at $3.10 — the day's total is $21.50 and a notification arrives: «Daily budget exceeded: spent $21.50 today (alert at $20.00)». Later runs that day do not alert again.",
} as Record<string, string>;
